lygon points="90,196 96.3,193 96.3,199" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="94.9" y1="196" x2="305.1" y2="196" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="310,196 303.7,199 303.7,193" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="200" y="208" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">5.0 cm</text><text x="200" y="22" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">E = 1.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹</text><line x1="20" y1="110" x2="79.7" y2="110" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="86,110 77.8,113.8 77.8,106.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="22" y="96" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹</text><polyline points="90,110 97.3,110 104.7,109.8 112,109.6 119.3,109.3 126.7,108.8 134,108.3 141.3,107.7 148.7,107 156,106.2 163.3,105.3 170.7,104.4 178,103.3 185.3,102.1 192.7,100.9 200,99.5 207.3,98.1 214.7,96.5 222,94.9 229.3,93.2 236.7,91.3 244,89.4 251.3,87.4 258.7,85.3 266,83.1 273.3,80.8 280.7,78.5 288,76 295.3,73.4 302.7,70.8 310,68" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/></svg>', 'An electron moving horizontally at 2.0 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹ enters the uniform field (1.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹) between horizontal plates 5.0 cm long and is deflected towards the positive plate.'),
('5f616c4e-c065-5879-9a31-a805b74064d5', 'a6c1f6cd-641d-4d35-8950-b05e6c80ecf5', 'ea6e0ded-f55f-54ce-803b-1143224c470a', 'a-level', '21.3', '9702 coursebook 21.3 · Uniform electric fields', 42, 42, 'Electrons are accelerated from rest through 1500 V. They then pass midway between two horizontal plates that are 4.0 cm long, 1.0 cm apart and have 60 V between them. (a) Calculate the speed of the electrons entering the plates. (b) Calculate the time spent between the plates. (c) Calculate the field strength and the vertical acceleration. (d) Calculate the vertical deflection on leaving the plates, in mm. Give only the answer to (d). Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.6 mm', 'mm', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) v = √(2 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 1500 ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹) = 2.30 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. (b) t = 0.040 ÷ 2.295 × 10⁷ = 1.74 × 10⁻⁹ s. (c) E = 60 ÷ 0.010 = 6000 V m⁻¹; a = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 6000 ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ = 1.05 × 10¹⁵ m s⁻². (d) y = ½at² = 0.5 × 1.054 × 10¹⁵ × (1.743 × 10⁻⁹)² = 0.00160 m. In mm: 0.001600 × 1000 = 1.60. Mark scheme (7 marks). C1: ½mv² = eV. C1: t = L/v. C1: E = V/d. C1: a = eE/m. M1: y = ½at². C1: conversion to mm. A1: y = 1.60 mm.', 7, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 220" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgb216a298-t fgb216a298-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgb216a298-t">Electron beam between plates</title><desc id="fgb216a298-d">Electrons accelerated through 1500 V pass midway between horizontal plates 4.0 cm long and 1.0 cm apart with 60 V between them.</desc><rect x="90" y="42" width="220" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="90" y="170" width="220" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="78" y="46" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">+</text><text x="78" y="174" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">−</text><line x1="110" y1="56" x2="110" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="110,164 107,157.7 113,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="140" y1="56" x2="140" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="140,164 137,157.7 143,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="170" y1="56" x2="170" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="170,164 167,157.7 173,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="200" y1="56" x2="200" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="200,164 197,157.7 203,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="230" y1="56" x2="230" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="230,164 227,157.7 233,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="260" y1="56" x2="260" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="260,164 257,157.7 263,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="290" y1="56" x2="290" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="290,164 287,157.7 293,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><polygon points="332,50 335,56.3 329,56.3" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="332" y1="54.9" x2="332" y2="165.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="332,170 329,163.7 335,163.7" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="338" y="110" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">1.0 cm</text><polygon points="90,196 96.3,193 96.3,199" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="94.9" y1="196" x2="305.1" y2="196" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="310,196 303.7,199 303.7,193" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="200" y="208" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">4.0 cm</text><text x="200" y="22" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">60 V</text><line x1="20" y1="110" x2="79.7" y2="110" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="86,110 77.8,113.8 77.8,106.2" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><text x="22" y="96" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">electrons</text><polyline points="90,110 97.3,110 104.7,109.8 112,109.6 119.3,109.3 126.7,108.8 134,108.3 141.3,107.7 148.7,107 156,106.2 163.3,105.3 170.7,104.4 178,103.3 185.3,102.1 192.7,100.9 200,99.5 207.3,98.1 214.7,96.5 222,94.9 229.3,93.2 236.7,91.3 244,89.4 251.3,87.4 258.7,85.3 266,83.1 273.3,80.8 280.7,78.5 288,76 295.3,73.4 302.7,70.8 310,68" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round;stroke-dasharray:6 4"/></svg>', 'Electrons accelerated through 1500 V pass midway between horizontal plates 4.0 cm long and 1.0 cm apart with 60 V between them.'),
('b0c1d17c-4157-5b27-bafa-418e41e74a93', 'a6c1f6cd-641d-4d35-8950-b05e6c80ecf5', 'ea6e0ded-f55f-54ce-803b-1143224c470a', 'a-level', '21.3', '9702 coursebook 21.3 · Uniform electric fields', 43, 43, 'Before a lightning strike, the p.d. between a cloud base and the ground 2.0 km below is 1.0 × 10⁸ V. (a) Calculate the average field strength between the cloud and the ground. (b) Air breaks down at about 3.0 × 10⁶ V m⁻¹. Suggest why lightning can still occur. (c) The flash transfers 20 C through the full p.d. Calculate the energy transferred. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2e9', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = V/d = 1.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 2000 = 5.0 × 10⁴ V m⁻¹. (b) The field is far from uniform: near raindrops, pointed objects and the tip of the developing discharge it is much stronger, so the air ionises step by step. (c) W = QV = 20 × 1.0 × 10⁸ = 2.0 × 10⁹ J. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: E = V/d. B1: local field much higher than average. C1: W = QV. C1: substitution. A1: W = 2.0 × 10⁹ J.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('61af8b22-8034-5280-aca5-c5c6fc773d46', 'a6c1f6cd-641d-4d35-8950-b05e6c80ecf5', 'ea6e0ded-f55f-54ce-803b-1143224c470a', 'a-level', '21.3', '9702 coursebook 21.3 · Uniform electric fields', 44, 44, 'An electron leaves the positive plate of a pair of parallel plates with a speed of 4.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹, moving straight towards the negative plate. The plates are 2.0 cm apart with 30 V between them. (a) Calculate the initial kinetic energy of the electron. (b) Calculate the work it must do against the field to reach the negative plate. (c) State whether it reaches the negative plate and calculate its speed there. Give only the speed from (c). Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.337e6', 'm s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E_k = ½ × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × (4.0 × 10⁶)² = 7.29 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. (b) W = eV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 30 = 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. (c) E_k is larger than W, so it arrives with E_k = 7.288 × 10⁻¹⁸ − 4.8 × 10⁻¹⁸ = 2.49 × 10⁻¹⁸ J. v = √(2 × 2.488 × 10⁻¹⁸ ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹) = 2.34 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: ½mv². C1: W = eV. B1: compares energies, reaches plate. C1: KE remaining. C1: speed from KE. A1: v = 2.34 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 220" role="img" aria-labelledby="fgdaae9c93-t fgdaae9c93-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fgdaae9c93-t">Electron leaving the positive plate</title><desc id="fgdaae9c93-d">Plates 2.0 cm apart with 30 V between them; an electron leaves the positive (top) plate at 4.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹, moving straight towards the negative plate.</desc><rect x="90" y="42" width="220" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="90" y="170" width="220" height="8" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="78" y="46" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">+</text><text x="78" y="174" font-size="14" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor;font-weight:600">−</text><line x1="110" y1="56" x2="110" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="110,164 107,157.7 113,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="140" y1="56" x2="140" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="140,164 137,157.7 143,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="170" y1="56" x2="170" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="170,164 167,157.7 173,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="200" y1="56" x2="200" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="200,164 197,157.7 203,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="230" y1="56" x2="230" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="230,164 227,157.7 233,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="260" y1="56" x2="260" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="260,164 257,157.7 263,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="290" y1="56" x2="290" y2="159.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="290,164 287,157.7 293,157.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><polygon points="332,50 335,56.3 329,56.3" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><line x1="332" y1="54.9" x2="332" y2="165.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="332,170 329,163.7 335,163.7" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280);stroke:none"/><text x="338" y="110" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">2.0 cm</text><text x="200" y="22" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">30 V</text><circle cx="200" cy="62" r="8" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="200" y="62.5" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);font-weight:700">−</text><text x="212" y="50" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">4.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹, moving down</text></svg>', 'Plates 2.0 cm apart with 30 V between them; an electron leaves the positive (top) plate at 4.0 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹, moving straight towards the negative plate.'),
('7b1081e6-5e4f-58b5-9a9c-57725393e590', 'a6c1f6cd-641d-4d35-8950-b05e6c80ecf5', 'ea6e0ded-f55f-54ce-803b-1143224c470a', 'a-level', '21.3', '9702 coursebook 21.3 · Uniform electric fields', 45, 45, 'A small charged sphere hangs between two vertical parallel plates 5.0 cm apart. The horizontal force on it is measured for different p.d.s: V/V = 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000 and F/mN = 0.85, 1.70, 2.55, 3.40. (a) Describe the relationship between F and V. (b) Calculate the gradient of the graph of F against V. (c) Use the gradient to calculate the charge on the sphere, in nC. (d) Suggest why the sphere should be kept near the middle of the plates. Give only the answer to (c).', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '42.5 nC', 'nC', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) F is proportional to V: a straight line through the origin. (b) Gradient = (3.40 − 0.85) × 10⁻³ ÷ (4000 − 1000) = 8.50 × 10⁻⁷ N V⁻¹. (c) F = QV/d, so gradient = Q/d and Q = 8.500 × 10⁻⁷ × 0.050 = 4.25 × 10⁻⁸ C. In nC: 4.250 × 10⁻⁸ × 10⁹ = 42.5. (d) The field is uniform only away from the edges; near a plate the sphere also induces charge on it. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: F ∝ V. C1: gradient. M1: gradient = Q/d. C1: conversion to nC. B1: uniform field in centre. A1: Q = 42.5 nC.', 6, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 400 410" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg42e36161-t fg42e36161-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg42e36161-t">Force against p.d.</title><desc id="fg42e36161-d">Table and graph of the horizontal force on a charged sphere between plates 5.0 cm apart against p.d.: V = 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000 V give F = 0.85, 1.70, 2.55, 3.40 mN, a straight line through the origin.</desc><g transform="translate(0,0)"><rect x="135.3" y="8" width="129.3" height="140" rx="0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><rect x="135.3" y="8" width="129.3"